winewayland: anchor an unowned popup to the toplevel its thread's input is in, and adopt the compositor's slide for it
(neutron popup-input-anchor; kill switch NEUTRON_POPUP_INPUT_ANCHOR=0)

What he saw (runtime 11.10-186, Photoshop 2026, KDE Plasma 6.8 Wayland, 175%, two screens): Edit > Color Settings > RGB
working space: "it lists them but it's broken as fuck. It closes instantly after you click it and it changes the color space.
You have to hold the click to keep it up." Edit > Convert to Profile > Profile: "the mouse target is way off. It highlights
options way above the cursor". The 4-item Intent list in the same dialog: "yes, those are fine".

Mechanism (SOLID, lab trace + window rects). The list is a ComboLBox with NO Win32 owner. win32u hands the driver owner_hint =
root of the window under the list's top-left corner. CBDropDown puts a list that fits neither below nor above the combo at the
top of the work area (as on Windows), i.e. above the dialog, so the window under its corner is Photoshop's MAIN window and the
list became an xdg_popup of the main window at Win32-absolute offsets. The main window is where Win32 thinks; the dialog is not
(KWin placed Color Settings at y 349.6 logical, Win32 has it at 216: Wayland never tells a client where a toplevel is). The
combo, the pointer (surface-local to the dialog + the dialog's Win32 origin) and the list's hit-testing are in the dialog's
frame, the list's pixels in the main window's: every point is off by 133 px -- the highlighted row sits 133.2 px above the
cursor and the release picks it. A short list's corner is inside the dialog, so it was anchored to the dialog and was right.
Present on 171 already (same list, same offset): not new in 185.

Windows: positions are absolute and every window is where its app put it, so any frame gives the same answer. Here only the
frame the app computed the rect in is right. An unowned popup is placed from the window the user is working in (a combo list
from its combo, which holds the capture; an autocomplete list from its focused edit) -- Win32 records that as the thread's
capture / focus / active window (GUITHREADINFO). Such a popup is now anchored to that window's root. When every toplevel is
where Win32 thinks, this is a different frame with the same answer. Part 2: the app sized the list to fit the work area in the
dialog's frame; in the real one it runs off the bottom, and the positioner's slide brings it back -- so the Win32 window is
moved to where the compositor put it (as GetWindowRect reports an adjusted window on Windows), as a delta from where the
positioner asked, applied to the raw rect; a 1-unit difference is rounding at a fractional scale and is ignored; bounded (3).
Windows with a Win32 owner (menus, tooltips, owned dialogs) and tray popups are untouched.

Not covered: (1) a QUICK click still closes the list and selects the item now under the cursor (fix 2/2, kill switch 3/3):
a separate mechanism, wineserver's synthetic WM_MOUSEMOVE (set_window_pos -> update_cursor_pos, for ANY visible window whose
rect changes, even far from the cursor) arrives right after the list shows and changes its frame; the combo (holding the
capture) sees the cursor inside the list and hands tracking to it, so the button-up selects. Whether Windows does the same is
UNKNOWN at the time; the Windows probe rounds that followed are covered by the combo-handover patch. (2) A residual of ~1-2 logical px (integer
surface coordinates at 175%): at a row edge the neighbor row can be the one picked. (3) Keyboard-opened lists (alt+Down) use
the focus window -- covered by the same rule, checked only by the regression arm's capture.

Lab evidence (Photoshop 2026, fresh reflink of PgD-ps2 at 168 dpi, desk p6a 175%/200%, press-and-hold 6 s on the RGB combo,
highlighted row measured against the cursor): 186 and 171: -133.2 px (n=2 + n=1); 11.10-186pp (this patch): the row at the
cursor (f1, f2: row 432..448 logical, cursor 449); same build + NEUTRON_POPUP_INPUT_ANCHOR=0: -133.2 px (k1, k3). Trace
(NEUTRON_POPUP_TRACE=1): fix = owner is the dialog, KWin slid it to y 54.3, Win32 rect moved 56 -> -138 raw; kill = owner is
the main window. Short Intent list: open and in place on all arms.
Regression (11.10-186pp vs the same build + NEUTRON_POPUP_INPUT_ANCHOR=0, n=1 each, integ186's scripted arms): Photoshop
(File/Edit menus, document, Camera Raw, Color Settings, Convert to Profile, preferences, type, maximize), Illustrator, Premiere
(3 menu passes) and Lightroom Classic (soft-proof Profile list, masking, export): menus open with identical pixel counts,
popup role/owner decisions identical apart from the combo lists, owned subsurfaces identical (PS 3, LrC 1); frame differences =
timing noise (Premiere playhead, PS type bar) and LrC's left-panel scroll-bar column (known startup race, not this patch).
Wine-native combos (probe, comctl32 v6 and user32): the long list is anchored to its dialog and the hit item is the one under
the cursor (item 47) vs item 35 with the kill switch. Not run: right-click menus.
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
@@ -470,6 +470,11 @@
      * a slid panel jumped 491x125 device px (289x74 logical) with 4 ms between samples. */
     int32_t popup_slide_x, popup_slide_y;
     BOOL popup_slide_pending;
+    /* NEUTRON popup-input-anchor (window.c neutron_popup_input_anchor): this popup has no Win32 owner and is
+     * anchored to the toplevel its thread's input is in; popup_expect_x/y = where the last positioner asked
+     * for it (owner window-geometry relative, surface units), so a configure elsewhere is the compositor's slide. */
+    BOOL input_anchor;
+    int32_t popup_expect_x, popup_expect_y;
     /* NEUTRON tray-popup-layer: WAYLAND_SURFACE_ROLE_LAYER, at margins (output-local logical) from the top-left of
      * layer_output (see wayland_surface_make_layer) */
     struct zwlr_layer_surface_v1 *layer_surface;
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -906,6 +906,9 @@
                 ERR("Failed to create positioner!\n");
                 return;
             }
+            /* NEUTRON popup-input-anchor: where this positioner puts the popup if nothing is constrained */
+            surface->popup_expect_x = owner->geometry.left + config.x;
+            surface->popup_expect_y = owner->geometry.top + config.y;
             xdg_popup_reposition(surface->xdg_popup, xdg_positioner, 0);
             xdg_positioner_destroy(xdg_positioner);
             wl_surface_commit(surface->wl_surface);
@@ -933,6 +936,9 @@
                                        &config.width, &config.height);
 
     if (!(xdg_positioner = popup_create_positioner(owner, &config))) goto err;
+    /* NEUTRON popup-input-anchor: where this positioner puts the popup if nothing is constrained */
+    surface->popup_expect_x = owner->geometry.left + config.x;
+    surface->popup_expect_y = owner->geometry.top + config.y;
 
     surface->xdg_popup = xdg_surface_get_popup(surface->xdg_surface, owner->xdg_surface,
                                                xdg_positioner);
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -1316,6 +1316,73 @@
     return (ex & WS_EX_LAYERED) && ((ex & WS_EX_TRANSPARENT) || (st & WS_DISABLED)) &&
            ((ex & WS_EX_NOACTIVATE) || !(ex & WS_EX_TOPMOST));
 }
+/* NEUTRON popup-input-anchor (NEUTRON_POPUP_INPUT_ANCHOR=0 disables).
+ *
+ * WHAT HE SAW (Photoshop 2026, 11.10-186, two screens at 175%): the RGB working-space list in Edit > Color Settings
+ * and the Profile list in Edit > Convert to Profile "closes instantly after you click it and it changes the color
+ * space", and while the button is held it "highlights options way above the cursor". The 4-item Intent list in the
+ * same dialog is fine.
+ *
+ * WHY. An unmanaged window becomes an xdg_popup of `owner`, at its Win32 position RELATIVE to that owner's Win32
+ * position. That is exact only if the owner is where Win32 thinks it is -- and on Wayland no toplevel is guaranteed
+ * to be: KWin placed Color Settings 133 logical px lower than the position Photoshop gave it (the compositor
+ * places xdg_toplevels; nothing tells Wine). Within one toplevel this never shows, because the pointer, the
+ * window and its popups all share that toplevel's frame. The combo list (ComboLBox) has NO Win32 owner, so the
+ * owner it got was win32u's owner_hint fallback: the root window under its top-left corner. The tall list is put
+ * at the top of the work area by CBDropDown (exactly what Windows does: it does not fit below the combo), above
+ * the dialog -- so the window under its corner was the MAIN window, the list landed in the main window's frame
+ * while the combo, the pointer (implicit grab on the dialog's surface) and the list's hit-testing were all in the
+ * dialog's: every point off by the dialog's misplacement. A short list's corner is inside the dialog, so it is
+ * anchored to the dialog and was always right.
+ *
+ * On Windows every window is where the app put it, so any frame gives the same answer. Here only one frame is
+ * right: the one the app computed the rect in. An unowned popup's rect is computed from the window the user is
+ * working in -- a combo list from its combo (CBDropDown: GetWindowRect of the combo, with the combo holding the
+ * capture), an autocomplete list from its edit box -- which Win32 records as the thread's capture, focus and
+ * active window (GUITHREADINFO). So such a popup is anchored to the root of that window. When every toplevel is
+ * where Win32 thinks, this chooses a different frame with the same answer; it changes something only when a
+ * toplevel has been placed elsewhere, and then it picks the frame the coordinates came from.
+ *
+ * Not used: windows with a Win32 owner (menus, tooltips, owned dialogs keep their owner), a tray popup (it has
+ * its own layer path), a candidate that is the popup itself or owned by it. Returns TRUE when it chose. */
+static BOOL neutron_popup_input_anchor_on(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) on = neutron_gate("NEUTRON_POPUP_INPUT_ANCHOR", TRUE);
+    return on;
+}
+
+static BOOL neutron_popup_input_anchor(HWND hwnd, HWND *owner)
+{
+    GUITHREADINFO info;
+    HWND cand[3], root;
+    DWORD style, exstyle;
+    int i;
+
+    if (!neutron_popup_input_anchor_on()) return FALSE;
+    if (NtUserGetWindowRelative(hwnd, GW_OWNER)) return FALSE;
+    style = NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_STYLE);
+    exstyle = NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE);
+    if (wayland_tray_popup_wanted(hwnd, style, exstyle)) return FALSE;
+    memset(&info, 0, sizeof(info));
+    info.cbSize = sizeof(info);
+    if (!NtUserGetGUIThreadInfo(NtUserGetWindowThread(hwnd, NULL), &info)) return FALSE;
+    cand[0] = info.hwndCapture;
+    cand[1] = info.hwndFocus;
+    cand[2] = info.hwndActive;
+    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)
+    {
+        if (!cand[i] || !(root = NtUserGetAncestor(cand[i], GA_ROOT)) || root == hwnd) continue;
+        if (!NtUserIsWindowVisible(root) || NtUserGetWindowRelative(root, GW_OWNER) == hwnd) continue;
+        if (root != *owner)
+            TRACE("hwnd=%p anchored to %p (input window %p, %s) instead of owner_hint %p\n", hwnd, root, cand[i],
+                  i == 0 ? "capture" : i == 1 ? "focus" : "active", *owner);
+        *owner = root;
+        return TRUE;
+    }
+    return FALSE;
+}
+
 void WAYLAND_WindowPosChanged(HWND hwnd, HWND insert_after, HWND owner_hint, UINT swp_flags,
                               const struct window_rects *new_rects, struct window_surface *surface)
 {
@@ -1329,6 +1396,7 @@
     struct wayland_client_surface *client;
     struct wayland_win_data *data, *toplevel_data, *owner_data;
     BOOL managed, fullscreen = swp_flags & WINE_SWP_FULLSCREEN;
+    BOOL input_anchor = FALSE;   /* NEUTRON popup-input-anchor */
 
     TRACE("hwnd %p new_rects %s after %p flags %08x\n", hwnd, debugstr_window_rects(new_rects), insert_after, swp_flags);
 
@@ -1351,6 +1419,8 @@
      * may need to query win_data information about other HWNDs and thus
      * acquire the lock itself internally. */
     if (!(managed = is_window_managed(hwnd, swp_flags, fullscreen)) && surface) owner = owner_hint;
+    /* NEUTRON popup-input-anchor: an unowned popup belongs to the frame its thread's input is in, see above */
+    if (!managed && surface) input_anchor = neutron_popup_input_anchor(hwnd, &owner);
     owner_chain_len = neutron_owner_chain(owner, owner_chain);
 
     if (!(data = wayland_win_data_get(hwnd))) return;
@@ -1579,6 +1649,9 @@
     {
         wayland_win_data_update_wayland_state(data);
     }
+    /* NEUTRON popup-input-anchor: let the slide handler know this popup's frame is the input window's */
+    if (data->wayland_surface && wayland_surface_is_popup(data->wayland_surface))
+        data->wayland_surface->input_anchor = input_anchor && owner_surface;
 
     /* An owned top-level window that is a subsurface of its owner: restack below if its place
      * in the owner's stack can have changed (shown, hidden, raised/lowered) or its wl_subsurface
@@ -1625,6 +1698,60 @@
  * gives the compositor a fresh chance to slide it; a popup wedged against an edge could ping-pong
  * forever. Converges in one step in the normal case (we move exactly where it asked), so a small
  * cap costs nothing and makes an oscillation impossible. */
+/* NEUTRON popup-input-anchor, part 2: a popup anchored by neutron_popup_input_anchor() that the compositor SLID.
+ *
+ * The app sized and placed the popup to fit the work area in its own frame -- the combo list in Color Settings
+ * fills the work area from its top. When that frame (the dialog) really sits lower, the same offset runs off the
+ * bottom of the screen, and the positioner's slide (popup_create_positioner) brings it back up: on screen, but no
+ * longer where Win32 has it, so the pointer and the list disagree by the slide distance again. On Windows a
+ * window is where its rect says; the faithful answer is to move the Win32 window to where it really is, as
+ * GetWindowRect reports an adjusted menu on Windows. Measured as a DELTA from where the positioner asked
+ * (popup_expect_x/y), so the owner's window geometry and content origin cancel out; applied to the RAW rect.
+ *
+ * A difference of one surface unit is the rounding of window px -> surface units at a fractional scale, not a
+ * slide: ignored (wayland_popup_adopt_slide below chased exactly that on Premiere). Bounded like it, too.
+ * Returns FALSE when the popup is not one of ours (the older, gated adoption below then decides). */
+static BOOL neutron_popup_adopt_input_slide(HWND hwnd)
+{
+    struct wayland_win_data *data;
+    struct wayland_surface *surface;
+    int dx, dy;
+    RECT rect;
+    double scale;
+
+    if (!(data = wayland_win_data_get(hwnd))) return FALSE;
+    if (!(surface = data->wayland_surface) || !wayland_surface_is_popup(surface) || !surface->input_anchor ||
+        !surface->popup_slide_pending)
+    {
+        wayland_win_data_release(data);
+        return FALSE;
+    }
+    surface->popup_slide_pending = FALSE;
+    dx = surface->popup_slide_x - surface->popup_expect_x;
+    dy = surface->popup_slide_y - surface->popup_expect_y;
+    if (abs(dx) <= 1) dx = 0;
+    if (abs(dy) <= 1) dy = 0;
+    if ((!dx && !dy) || surface->popup_slide_applied >= 3)
+    {
+        if (dx || dy) WARN("popup %p slid %u times already; not chasing it further\n", hwnd, surface->popup_slide_applied);
+        wayland_win_data_release(data);
+        return TRUE;
+    }
+    surface->popup_slide_applied++;
+    surface->popup_expect_x = surface->popup_slide_x;
+    surface->popup_expect_y = surface->popup_slide_y;
+    scale = surface->window.scale;
+    rect = surface->window.rect;
+    /* ⛔ released before NtUserSetRawWindowPos: it re-enters wayland_win_data_get (round-34 ABBA) */
+    wayland_win_data_release(data);
+
+    TRACE("hwnd=%p slid by (%d,%d) surface units at scale %.4f: moving %s by (%d,%d)\n", hwnd, dx, dy, scale,
+          wine_dbgstr_rect(&rect), (int)round(dx * scale), (int)round(dy * scale));
+    OffsetRect(&rect, (int)round(dx * scale), (int)round(dy * scale));
+    NtUserSetRawWindowPos(hwnd, rect, SWP_NOSIZE | SWP_NOZORDER | SWP_NOACTIVATE | SWP_NOOWNERZORDER, FALSE);
+    return TRUE;
+}
+
 static void wayland_popup_adopt_slide(HWND hwnd)
 {
     struct wayland_win_data *data;
@@ -2380,7 +2507,7 @@
         return 0;
 
     case WM_WAYLAND_POPUP_SLIDE:
-        wayland_popup_adopt_slide(hwnd);
+        if (!neutron_popup_adopt_input_slide(hwnd)) wayland_popup_adopt_slide(hwnd);
         return 0;
     case WM_WAYLAND_OUTPUT_CHANGE:
         wayland_window_output_change(hwnd, (UINT)wp);
